// EXPECT: runtime_fail
//ERR: Error: expected int in any, got str

// An unannotated return defaults to checked any (return-type
// inference does not exist); the any-held str traps at take's
// annotated int edge. Mechanism sibling: any_trap_str_int_fail
// (explicit `: any` decl); this file pins the checked-any DEFAULT as
// the provenance. Deliberately runtime_fail today: if flow typing
// ever lands, this may promote to type_fail -- re-decide then, do
// not silently move. Also pins that bare-call arg checking admits
// checked-any args: the bare call `take(v);` compiles and defers to
// take's entry unwrap -- over-strict checking would surface here as
// UNEXPECTED (compile failed).
fn make() {
    return "hello";
}
fn take(n: int) {
    print(n + 1);
}
var v <- make();
take(v);
